WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:05.560
After unveiling an epic sizzle reel for Star
Wars: The Rise of Skywalker at this year's

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:09.960
D23 Expo, LucasFilm has released the video
to the masses.

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:14.940
The special look is all sorts of incredible,
mostly due to its final few seconds, which

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:18.100
gave us the first official look at dark Rey.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:22.759
Dressed in a black hooded robe, Rey wields
a double-bladed red lightsaber, which she

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:26.620
flourishes downward to snap into a battle-ready
position.

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:31.860
Her skin appears far paler than normal, and
her eyes look sunken into her head.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:35.740
Suffice it to say, this isn't the Rey we're
used to seeing.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:37.920
But what's actually at play here?

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:42.579
Judging by the clothes Rey wears and the red
saber, one could argue that she has officially

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:46.620
turned to the dark side, joining Kylo Ren
and the First Order.

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:51.600
Study her face and her posture a bit more,
though, and you'll see some signs that suggest

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:53.760
dark Rey isn't the real Rey.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:59.179
Dark Rey is stiff when she handles the double-bladed
saber, whereas normally, Rey is swift when

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:00.580
working with her weapons.

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:06.280
Additionally, dark Rey is practically emotionless,
her face barely moves a millimeter, which

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:09.360
isn't typical of Rey's usually hyper-emotive
mug.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.400
So, what does it all mean?

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:13.940
Well, there are a few possibilities.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:18.660
This dark Rey shown in the new footage from
The Rise of Skywalker could be a clone of

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:23.040
the regular Rey, one of several that were
created at the time of Rey's "birth."

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:28.020
Considering The Rise of Skywalker will feature
a brand-new iteration of Stormtroopers, the

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:33.190
red-armored Sith Troopers, some theorize that
dark Rey and the entire fleet of Sith Troopers

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:34.720
are Rey clones.

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:39.200
This could tie into The Last Jedi, during
which Rey entered a cave and saw dozens of

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:40.620
copies of herself.

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:46.000
The idea goes that these multiple Reys weren't
just visions, they're real, which would help

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.560
explain why the Star Wars saga hasn't yet
fully explained Rey's parentage.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:55.360
Sure, Kylo Ren snapped at Rey and told her
that her parents were nobodies who sold her

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:59.920
for drinking money, but perhaps the First
Order edgelord made it up to upset her.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:05.040
Maybe, just maybe, Rey doesn't have any parents
at all…because she's one of a whole bunch

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:08.560
of clones made for the purpose of fighting
in the galactic war.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:13.780
Perhaps her "sisters" are all evil, while
Rey is the sole clone who malfunctioned and

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:15.280
joined the light side.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:16.920
Or maybe she's not a clone.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:22.220
Maybe she had a twin, with Rey's parents having
sold her sister immediately after their birth.

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:26.480
The abandoned twin might have grown vengeful
and turned to the dark side, and the evil

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:29.320
Rey we see could be that scorned sister.

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:34.040
Another theory is that dark Rey isn't real
in the slightest, and that what we're seeing

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:37.800
in the Rise of Skywalker clip is part of a
sort of dream sequence.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:42.600
Just as Luke Skywalker saw himself as his
father, Darth Vader, while inside the Dagobah

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:47.420
cave in The Empire Strikes Back, Rey could
have a similar vision in The Rise of Skywalker,

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:51.080
facing her biggest fear in watching herself
turn to the dark side.

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:55.080
This could give her the push she needs to
continue fighting against the First Order

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:58.500
when the temptation to turn dark grows too
strong.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:03.520
There are about a billion possible explanations
for what's going on here, and the same can

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:08.540
be said for what looks like an evil version
of C-3PO in the newly released Rise of Skywalker

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:09.440
footage.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:14.280
The protocol droid, known for his etiquette
and intelligence, is seen here with glowing

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:15.340
red eyes.

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:19.440
That's the only physical attribute of his
to suggest that the upcoming film features

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:24.870
a dark C-3PO, and while we hate to make sweeping
generalizations in saying that every character

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:29.480
with red eyes is evil, there's evidence to
suggest that this could be the case here.

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:32.780
"Don't say things like that."

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:38.300
In February 2015, Marvel Comics rolled out
a new Darth Vader series, which revealed in

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:42.810
issue number three that the heavy-breathing
dark-sider had in his entourage droids that

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:46.140
have the same design as C-3PO and R2-D2.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:52.700
There's 0-0-0, essentially an evil version
of C-3PO from a design perspective, and BT-1,

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:56.080
an assassin droid that recalls the design
of R2-D2.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:01.220
These robots are battle droids designed to
kill, which is a far cry from how helpful

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:03.660
3P0 and R2 generally are.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:08.640
Speculation has it that the red-eyed C-3PO
shown in the Rise of Skywalker footage could

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:14.220
actually be 0-0-0, and that the Skywalker
who rises in the new movie is Anakin, who

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:18.340
may return as a Force ghost with his literal
killing machines in tow.

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:22.620
Since additional footage from The Rise of
Skywalker shown at a Disney shareholders'

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:28.350
meeting in March 2019 reportedly featured
Kylo Ren opening a container that holds Darth

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:32.630
Vader's helmet, it's not completely out of
the realm of possibility that the villain

00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:37.160
formerly known as Anakin Skywalker could play
a part in the upcoming film.

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:42.200
On the other hand, maybe the explanation for
the "evil" C-3PO is simple.

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:47.580
Perhaps the reason why the droid has red eyes
during that scene is because he had his personality

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:52.650
inadvertently augmented to be extra aggressive
for the sake of comedy, sort of like what

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:55.700
happens with Data's "emotion chip" in Star
Trek.

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:58.920
"Yes, I hate this"

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:04.520
All this is to say that many mysteries still
surround The Rise of Skywalker, and hardly

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:07.260
any concrete answers are on hand right now.

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:11.480
Writer and director J.J. Abrams could have
taken the film in any number of different

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:16.320
directions, making it all the more difficult
to pin down which theories could be true and

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.180
which are total BS.

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:23.220
The Rise of Skywalker will surely answer the
big questions about dark Rey and the potentially

00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:28.620
dark C-3PO, but fans will have to be content
with waiting until the film's release on December

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:30.440
20 to learn the truth.

